Thread synchronization is the process of ensuring that multiple threads do not interfere with each other while accessing shared resources. When multiple threads access the same shared resource, the result can be unpredictable and incorrect. Thread synchronization is important to prevent race conditions, where the final result of a computation depends on the order in which the threads execute.

There are several ways to synchronize threads in Java, including using the synchronized keyword, using synchronized blocks, and using the `synchronized` method. Synchronized blocks ensure that only one thread can execute a block of code at a time, while synchronized methods ensure that only one thread can execute a method at a time.

